  "slug": "furcellaria-genome-red-6916",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and a published study extend genomic, epigenetic and viral characterization of marine algae and their associated viruses.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these items is the genomic and genetic characterization of algae and their viruses, with attention to genome architecture, epigenetic machinery and viral communities. A de novo long-read reference genome of the red seaweed Furcellaria lumbricalis provides an assembly of 918.2 Mb across 149 contigs, with 6100 annotated genes and 90.5% repetitive sequence. In kelps (Laminariales), native-DNA nanopore sequencing detected no nuclear DNA methylation, supporting a predicted loss of canonical methyltransferases in brown algae. Viral studies extend this picture: an RNA virome from New Zealand intertidal kelp revealed novel viral lineages, with higher viral diversity at a city-proximal site and in unhealthy kelp, while long-read metagenomics of mesopelagic Andaman Sea seawater yielded 1,604 high-confidence viral contigs, mostly bacteriophages. Population-genetic work on the snow alga Chlainomonas sp. indicates a haploid-diploid life cycle with clonal reproduction and likely high selfing. What is new is the application of long-read and single-cell approaches to non-model algal and viral systems.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-24"
}
